    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me break it down for you in plain English.\n\nThe indemnification clause essentially means that if you (as the consultant) breach the agreement or perform services negligently, the client will not be liable for any damages or costs resulting from that breach. This includes any claims, damages, liabilities, costs, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys' fees) that the client might incur as a result.\n\nIn other words, if you do something wrong, the client will not be held responsible for paying for it. You, as the consultant, will be responsible for covering those costs.\n\nHowever, it's worth noting that this clause does not necessarily mean you can't be sued for things you didn't do. It's more about the client's liability being limited in case you breach the agreement or perform services negligently.\n\nTo illustrate, imagine you're a contractor hired to fix a leaky pipe. You accidentally make the leak worse, causing significant water damage to the client's property. The client might sue you for the damages, but the indemnification clause would protect them from having to pay for those damages. You, as the contractor, would be responsible for covering the costs.\n\nSo, to answer",
      "source": "target",
